  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "What is the coefficient of $(x+\\pi )^{3}$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ about $x=-\\pi ?$",
  "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $(x+\\pi )^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{(3)}(-\\pi ).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{\u2034}(-\\pi ),$ which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2034}(-\\pi )=-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-\\pi )=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3!}{f}^{\u2034}(-\\pi )=\\frac{1}{3!}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of $(x+\\pi )^{3}$ is $\\frac{1}{6}.$",
